{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfguimU9mshNvkQQSRUGi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new `AuditOwnerCreated` class itself is clean and idiomatic: one function per step, single public method, reads the persisted `Owner` via `@Val`, is well-documented, and is wired as its own step after `save` rather than being bolted onto `SaveOwner`. That is the right unit for this logic.\n\nThe one structural issue:\n\n- **`audit` step should not carry `govern: [ transaction ]`.** The audit step does no persistence — it only emits a log line. Per AGENTS.md, `govern: [ transaction ]` is for writes and should be \"listed on every step it covers,\" and governance spans the pipeline so the transaction commits after the last governed step. By adding `transaction` governance to a logging-only step you extend the open DB transaction to span the logging call, which is unnecessary and misleading about the step's intent. Previously the transaction closed after `save`; now it needlessly stays open through `audit`. Drop `govern: [ transaction ]` from the `audit` step (as `respond` already omits it) so the transaction scope ends at `save`.\n\nMinor (not blocking on its own): the method is named `service`, whereas the codebase's established convention names step methods after the verb they perform (`validate`, `load`, `apply`, `save`, `respond`). A name like `audit` would read more naturally and match the surrounding steps."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9392,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9392},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5f4cef7f-c344-4cb4-a331-7